Subject: Ft. Pierce Attached Files

I sent you and Kathleen the memo I sent to Rusty about confidentiality 
provisions in the FPUA/FPRP project documents.

In regard to disclosure on Deal Bench, I think you  need to consider 
carefully the confidentiality restrictions in place under binding agreements 
that restrict ENA's ability to disclose contracts and other information and 
the need to keep certain of the attachments to the E-Mail that Rusty sent you 
confidential. 

There are binding confidentiality provisions in place with almost every 
counterparty to the executed and proposed to be executed Fort Pierce project 
documents, some with exceptions that would permit disclosure to potential 
assignees/transferees, some without such exceptions. A systematic review of 
the confidentiality provisions in force to ascertain the scope of the 
confidentiality restrictions needs to be completed before Deal Bench 
disclosure, and to my knowledge that has not taken place. Also be advised 
that the Fort Pierce Development Agreements are not negotiated to finality or 
signed, and I am concerned that such disclosure at this time - if FPUA 
learned of it - could result in the deal never being signed and/or a 
potential purchaser being misled about the terms of the FPUA/FPRP 
transaction. 

Apart from the foregoing considerations, I have asked that the opinions and 
certificates attached to the E-Mail Rusty forwarded to you be kept strictly 
confidential. They will change, and there are matters in the certificates 
that we don't want advertised to FPUA, on Deal Bench, or otherwise.

Finally, I am not aware of the scope of ENA's confidentiality obligations in 
cases when it is negotiating documents in its capacity as agent for E-Next 
Generation LLC. By copy hereof, I'm requesting Lisa Bills and Rose Engeldorf 
advise us if there are such restrictions that would limit disclosure to 
potential purchasers independent of the other confidentiality restrictions 
referred to above.

In short, before any documents are disclosed to third parties via Deal Bench, 
these matters need to be resolved, and I frankly am too busy right now trying 
to get the deal with FPUA signed to make the review my priority. Perhaps we 
should start by having Kathleen take a look at the provisions in the various 
executed FPRP documents to catalog the various restrictions applicable to the 
proposed Deal Bench disclosure?

