uityAlert.com.     University of Dayton Selects Sybari's Antigen for Its Un=
matchable Protection of Domino Servers Against E-mail Borne Viruses and Mal=
icious Code=1DOne of Yahoo's Highest Ranking "Most Wired" Universities and =
Ohio's Largest Private University Deploys Antigen for Proactive E-mail Secu=
rity   E. NORTHPORT, N.Y., Nov 27, 2001 (BUSINESS WIRE) -- Sybari Software,=
 Inc., the premier developers of Antigen, a comprehensive antivirus, conten=
t-management, and e-mail security solution for Microsoft Exchange and Lotus=
 Domino environments, today announced that the University of Dayton (UD), w=
hich was recently ranked No.16 of 200 colleges and universities who were ac=
credited with the title of being one of Yahoo's Most Wired, has selected An=
tigen for antivirus and security protection of their Lotus Notes/Domino ser=
vers that support more than 10,000 students and nearly 2,000 faculty member=
s and administrative and research personnel.   "NIMDA," "SIRCAM," "LOVE," a=
nd "MELISSA" have clearly demonstrated how crippling a virus can be in term=
s of lost time, data, and productivity. The University of Dayton could not =
afford to join the growing number of universities and large organizations t=
hat have experienced the staggering costs of data recovery and downtime ass=
ociated with these and other malicious viruses. After conducting a full eva=
luation of server products from such companies as Trend Micro and McAfee, U=
D concluded that a server-level solution offering multiple scan engines and=
 superior file filtering capabilities, coupled with a strong vendor commitm=
ent to delivering a proactive line of defense from Internet and e-mail-born=
e viruses, would provide the level of protection UD needs today and in the =
future.   "We selected Sybari's Antigen for many reasons including trusted =
scanning, which means less processor time, the ability to quickly block any=
 files or file type, multiple scan engines, and the 24 x 7 uptime," said Su=
san McCabe, Director, Lotus Integration for the University of Dayton. "We d=
on't need to take our servers down to apply updates. Antigen truly gives us=
 all of the features we feel are important and delivers the best value."   =
Sybari's Antigen is the only solution with a choice of up to five leading s=
can engine technologies from: Network Associates, Norman Data Defense, Soph=
os, and two Computer Associates engines from their Etrust offering. Since n=
ew viruses may survive one engine, Antigen institutes an extra measure of p=
rotection by enabling users to run multiple-scan engines, virtually elimina=
ting network infection. Antigen comes equipped with Content Management faci=
lities that provide the proactive support needed to maintain a virus free g=
roupware environment. With the Antigen File Filter (AFF), administrators ca=
n filter e-mail attachments by file name, type, or wild card. A pre-emptive=
 content management feature like this can prove extremely useful while wait=
ing for signature files from anti-virus vendors with proprietary engines an=
d virus labs.   "We pioneered this industry with our flagship release of An=
tigen for Lotus Notes/Domino environments in the nineties," said Rocco Donn=
ino, vice president of sales, the Americas, for Sybari Software. "Our lates=
t release of Antigen further validates our commitment to developing highly =
specialized solutions for messaging platforms and its selection by such a p=
restigious university confirms our success in turning out winning solutions=
."   ABOUT SYBARI  Since 1995, Sybari has the led the market in providing i=
nnovative solutions to groupware-based virus and security threats. Today, o=
ver 5 million Microsoft Exchange/Outlook and Lotus Domino/Notes seats are v=
irus-free as a direct result of Sybari's Antigen technology. Sybari's Antig=
en is unsurpassed in providing protection of corporate messaging environmen=
ts. Antigen's unique architecture institutes a preemptive line of defense f=
rom viruses and malicious code. Antigen for Microsoft Exchange and Antigen =
for Lotus Domino are distributed in more than 50 countries through Sybari's=
 worldwide locations and distribution network. Sybari is headquartered in E=
ast Northport, New York with an EMEA headquarters in Madrid, Spain and an A=
sia Pacific Headquarters in Singapore. Sybari's clients include IBM, Amazon=
.com, Cable & Wireless, Compaq, Con Edison, Dell, Deloitte & Touche, Eastma=
n Chemical, Getronics, JD Power, Lufthansa, Mayo Foundation, Merrill Lynch,=
 Nortel, Pirelli, Reckitt Benckiser, Sony, Target, Texaco, Tosco, Union Pac=
ific, US Federal Government, and Visa. Sybari's many strategic partners inc=
lude Lotus Development (NYSE:IBM), Microsoft (NASDAQ:MSFT), Computer Associ=
ates (NYSE:CA), Compaq (NYSE:CPQ), and Sun Microsystems (NASDAQ:SUNW).   Ab=
out The University of Dayton  The University of Dayton (UD) is one of the n=
ation's largest Catholic universities and Ohio's largest private university=
, with an enrollment of more than 10,000 students, including more than 6,40=
0 full-time undergraduates. Founded in 1850 by the Society of Mary (Mariani=
sts), a Roman Catholic teaching order of priests and brothers, The Universi=
ty of Dayton is a residential learning community with more than 70 academic=
 programs in arts and sciences, business administration, education and alli=
ed professions, engineering and law. The University of Dayton was recently =
named a national doctoral university and ranks among the top Catholic unive=
rsities and colleges in the country, according to the new issue of America'=
s Best Colleges from U.S. News and World Report. UD was previously included=
 in the top 5 list of the best regional colleges in the Midwest, but its wi=
de range of doctoral programs and other features now qualify it as a nation=
al university under new classification guidelines by the Carnegie Foundatio=
n for Advancement in Teaching.   Other products and company names mentioned=
